!script {{
  --/| ==========================================================================
  --/| Tutorial 05: Finding Specific D&D 2024 Beacon Repeating Rows
  --/|
  --/| PURPOSE
  --/| Tutorial 04 showed how to walk through every row with Rfirst and Rnext.
  --/| Often a script already knows which row it wants.
  --/|
  --/| This tutorial demonstrates four ways to load a particular repeating row:
  --/|
  --/| Rfind         finds a row by an exact field value.
  --/| Rsearch       finds a row by a pattern.
  --/| Rbyindex      loads a row by its position in the section.
  --/| Rbysectionid  loads a row by its saved row ID.
  --/|
  --/| HOW TO USE IT
  --/| 1. Select a D&D 2024 Beacon NPC with at least one normal Action.
  --/| 2. Run this ScriptCard.
  --/| 3. The script first saves information from a real Action so the later
  --/|    examples do not depend on a hard-coded action name.
  --/|
  --/| Rfind VS Rsearch
  --/| Rfind is useful when you know the exact value you want.
  --/|
  --/| Rsearch uses regular-expression pattern matching. You do not need to know
  --/| regular expressions to understand this example: .+ simply means
  --/| "one or more characters," so it finds the first non-empty Action name.
  --/|
  --/| INDEXES AND ROW IDS
  --/| Index 0 means the first row, index 1 means the second row, and so on.
  --/|
  --/| A saved row ID is better when you need to return to the exact same row
  --/| later, because positions can change if rows are added or reordered.
  --/|
  --/| CLASSIC REPEATING ROWS VS BEACON REPEATING ROWS
  --/| Classic sheets use traditional repeating Attribute objects. Beacon stores
  --/| native records. ScriptCards presents compatible repeating rows in Beacon
  --/| mode so these familiar commands can still be used.
  --/|
  --/| This tutorial only reads information. It does not change the character.
  --/| ==========================================================================

  --#beaconsheet|1
  --#sourcetoken|@{selected|token_id}
  --#title|[*S:character_name] - Finding Repeating Rows Tutorial

  --&CharacterID|[*S:character_id]
  --&Section|repeating_npcaction
  --&RowCount|[*R:[&CharacterID]:[&Section]:rowcount]

  --+|[c][b]Section Overview[/b][/c]

  --+Section|[&Section]
  --+Number of Rows|[&RowCount]

  --?"[&RowCount]" -eq "0"|NoRows

  --+|[c][b]Save an Example Row[/b][/c]

  --Rfirst|[&CharacterID];[&Section]
  --&SavedName|[*R:name]
  --&SavedID|[*R:xxxActionIDxxxx]
  --&SavedDescription|[*R:description]

  --+Example Name|[&SavedName]
  --+Example Row ID|[&SavedID]
  --+Example Description|[&SavedDescription]

  --+|[c][b]Rfind: Exact Field Value[/b][/c]

  --/| Search the name field for the exact name saved above.
  --Rfind|[&CharacterID];[&SavedName];[&Section];name

  --+Found Name|[*R:name]
  --+Found Row ID|[*R:xxxActionIDxxxx]

  --+|[c][b]Rsearch: Pattern Match[/b][/c]

  --/| .+ means one or more characters. This finds the first Action whose name
  --/| is not empty.
  --Rsearch|[&CharacterID];.+;[&Section];name

  --+Matched Name|[*R:name]
  --+Matched Row ID|[*R:xxxActionIDxxxx]

  --+|[c][b]Rbyindex: Zero-Based Position[/b][/c]

  --Rbyindex|[&CharacterID];[&Section];0

  --+Index Used|0
  --+Loaded Name|[*R:name]
  --+Loaded Row ID|[*R:xxxActionIDxxxx]

  --+|[c][b]Rbysectionid: Saved Row ID[/b][/c]

  --Rbysectionid|[&CharacterID];[&Section];[&SavedID]

  --+Saved ID Used|[&SavedID]
  --+Loaded Name|[*R:name]
  --+Loaded Description|[*R:description]

  --+|[c][b]Read by Index Without Loading the Row[/b][/c]

  --+First Row Name|[*R:[&CharacterID]:[&Section]:0:name]
  --+First Row ID|[*R:[&CharacterID]:[&Section]:0:xxxActionIDxxxx]

  --+|[c][b]Finished[/b][/c]

  --+Result|The same section was accessed by exact value, pattern, index, row ID, and direct indexed reference.
  --X|

  --:NoRows|
  --+|[c][b]No Action Rows Found[/b][/c]
  --+Result|Select an NPC with at least one normal Action and run the tutorial again.
}}
